From d7b2f90595441c4b1f8f869f73740a3d927711b1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Carson Daniel <wcarsond@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 23:48:19 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 01/12] vulkan : two-device all-reduce for --split-mode tensor,
 meta all-reduce profiler

Vulkan has no device-to-device copy, so the meta backend's generic all-reduce
stops both devices several times per exchange. This adds a backend all-reduce
for exactly two Vulkan devices through a block of host memory both import
(VK_EXT_external_memory_host): each device copies its partial into its slot,
one submit+wait on both, then each copies the other's slot and adds it, queued
behind the next subgraph. Anything else falls back to the generic path;
GGML_VK_DISABLE_COMM=1 forces it for A/B.

ggml_vk_synchronize is split into a submit half and a wait half so both devices
can be submitted before either is waited on.

GGML_META_PROFILE=1 times the all-reduces between subgraphs in the meta backend
(count, microseconds, bytes, share of the graph), printed every 64 graphs.

Measured on two Arc Pro B60s, Windows 11, tg128 vs one card:
  granite 30B dense     generic 0.85x -> comm 1.12x
  qwen2.5-coder 14B     generic 0.65x -> comm 0.92x
  qwen3 30B-A3B MoE     generic 0.33x -> comm 0.49x
Per-exchange time 190 us -> 150 us; the remaining cost is the one stop.
